WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.117
An agency built a virtual team to produce market reports.

00:00:06.417 --> 00:00:07.408
Five agents.

00:00:07.708 --> 00:00:14.259
A senior researcher, a data analyst, a writer, a reviewer, and an editor in chief.

00:00:14.909 --> 00:00:16.833
Look at the configuration.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:20.004
Only the researcher has a tool, web search.

00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:21.973
All the others have none.

00:00:22.273 --> 00:00:25.090
And the flow is always the same sequence.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.491
The trace shows what happens.

00:00:27.791 --> 00:00:31.096
The researcher runs the searches, and writes a summary.

00:00:31.396 --> 00:00:34.655
The analyst rewrites the summary in other words.

00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:37.150
The writer turns it into a report.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.920
The reviewer changes a few sentences.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.625
And the editor replies: approved.

00:00:42.925 --> 00:00:47.219
Five large calls, each rereading the previous one's text.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:51.072
A single agent, with the search tool, delivers the same report.

00:00:51.372 --> 00:00:56.264
In this lesson, you'll learn to tell decoration from real division of work.

00:00:57.464 --> 00:00:58.543
Here's the shape.

00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:01.995
The only agent with a tool does the real work.

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:06.770
The others read the whole text, and return a similar version.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.486
And the result costs five times as much, and takes five times as long.

00:01:13.136 --> 00:01:14.125
How do you spot it?

00:01:14.425 --> 00:01:20.522
The agents have human job titles, like researcher, analyst or manager, instead of

00:01:20.522 --> 00:01:22.555
technical responsibilities.

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:25.263
Only one or two agents have tools.

00:01:25.563 --> 00:01:28.106
The others only read, and rewrite.

00:01:28.756 --> 00:01:33.329
The flow is always the same sequence, with no decision about who works.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:38.830
Each agent receives the previous one's entire text, and returns a similar text.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.009
And removing an agent doesn't change quality in the eval.

00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:46.566
While the cost is several times that of one call.

00:01:47.766 --> 00:01:51.997
The honest way to see this smell is to drop an agent, and measure.

00:01:52.297 --> 00:01:56.498
Say the agency's eval has forty reports, and a rubric.

00:01:56.798 --> 00:02:01.118
Claims with sources, numbers that match the page, the agreed structure.

00:02:01.768 --> 00:02:04.852
You turn off the reviewer and the editor in chief.

00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:06.462
The score doesn't move.

00:02:07.112 --> 00:02:08.711
You turn off the analyst.

00:02:09.011 --> 00:02:13.069
The writer works straight from the researcher's summary, and the score stays the

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:13.322
same.

00:02:13.972 --> 00:02:16.914
One agent that researches and writes is left.

00:02:17.214 --> 00:02:19.151
The other four were job titles.

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:24.199
And if the score drops when you remove someone, that split is paying for something.

00:02:24.499 --> 00:02:27.588
Then you name the technical reason, and keep only that.

00:02:28.788 --> 00:02:29.931
Here's one for you.

00:02:30.231 --> 00:02:35.356
You turn off the reviewer and the editor in chief, on an eval of forty reports.

00:02:35.656 --> 00:02:38.003
The rubric score doesn't change.

00:02:38.303 --> 00:02:40.494
What's the most useful diagnosis?

00:02:43.544 --> 00:02:45.836
Those two roles are decorative.

00:02:46.136 --> 00:02:49.792
They reread and rewrite, without doing any new work.

00:02:50.092 --> 00:02:51.920
Collapsing them is the next step.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.532
Not reinforcing the job title.

00:02:55.182 --> 00:03:00.608
A bigger model on the reviewer, with no criterion of its own, only makes the rewrite

00:03:00.608 --> 00:03:01.577
more expensive.

00:03:01.877 --> 00:03:06.420
And adding a sixth agent, an auditor, to catch what the reviewer misses, would be

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.271
the same smell, with a new badge.

00:03:09.471 --> 00:03:11.223
The metaphor is appealing.

00:03:11.523 --> 00:03:16.271
A team of agents is easy to explain, and looks good in a presentation.

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:20.512
A loop with two tools and a rubric in code, much less so.

00:03:21.162 --> 00:03:22.514
Frameworks encourage it.

00:03:22.814 --> 00:03:27.819
Many tutorials open with roles and goals written in HR language.

00:03:28.119 --> 00:03:33.640
You copy the example, swap the names, and in an afternoon you have five agents

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:34.207
running.

00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:35.626
The demo talks.

00:03:35.926 --> 00:03:37.576
The cost shows up next month.

00:03:38.226 --> 00:03:41.162
Splitting by human role, not by context.

00:03:41.462 --> 00:03:46.667
In a company, splitting by role makes sense, because people have memory, hours and a

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:47.287
specialty.

00:03:47.587 --> 00:03:50.449
For a model, the reasons to split are different.

00:03:50.749 --> 00:03:56.208
The context window, tool permissions, review that mustn't see the maker's reasoning,

00:03:56.208 --> 00:03:58.417
and work that can run in parallel.

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.377
And no eval.

00:04:00.677 --> 00:04:05.392
Without measuring, nobody notices that three of the five agents change nothing.

00:04:05.692 --> 00:04:09.319
The reviewer improved the tone is a feeling.

00:04:09.619 --> 00:04:12.957
The rubric says whether it improved what matters.

00:04:14.157 --> 00:04:14.952
The cost.

00:04:15.252 --> 00:04:20.685
Each agent rereads the previous work, so cost grows with the number of roles, not

00:04:20.685 --> 00:04:22.362
with the value delivered.

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:27.262
With illustrative numbers, the first call starts at three thousand tokens of input.

00:04:27.562 --> 00:04:31.231
Each later role receives the previous one's entire text.

00:04:31.531 --> 00:04:34.485
Five, seven, nine, eleven thousand.

00:04:34.785 --> 00:04:40.688
You pay for the same text several times, and what the extra buys is four rewrites.

00:04:40.988 --> 00:04:43.637
Prompt caching makes the repeated start cheaper.

00:04:43.937 --> 00:04:46.336
It doesn't erase the four extra calls.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:50.912
And latency adds up too, because the roles run in a queue.

00:04:51.212 --> 00:04:54.380
Quality can get worse, like a game of telephone.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.724
A correct number in the researcher's summary becomes a different number in the

00:04:59.724 --> 00:05:00.629
writer's text.

00:05:00.929 --> 00:05:05.673
And when the report comes out wrong, there are five prompts to investigate, and

00:05:05.673 --> 00:05:08.316
nobody knows at which pass the fact changed.

00:05:09.516 --> 00:05:12.815
The treatment is a refactoring called collapse agents.

00:05:13.115 --> 00:05:18.354
Merge the agents that only pass text along into one agent, with the right tools.

00:05:18.654 --> 00:05:21.906
And keep separate only what has a technical reason.

00:05:22.206 --> 00:05:23.904
Those reasons do exist.

00:05:24.554 --> 00:05:26.246
Context isolation.

00:05:26.546 --> 00:05:31.093
Whoever reads forty pages shouldn't write the report in that same context.

00:05:31.743 --> 00:05:33.110
Restricted tools.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:37.704
Whoever summarizes a lead's website shouldn't hold the send email tool.

00:05:38.354 --> 00:05:39.516
Independent review.

00:05:39.816 --> 00:05:44.397
Whoever scores doesn't see the writer's reasoning, and the eval shows a gain.

00:05:45.047 --> 00:05:50.394
And parallelism, like twenty different repositories explored in twenty separate

00:05:50.394 --> 00:05:51.003
contexts.

00:05:51.653 --> 00:05:52.342
Quick check.

00:05:52.642 --> 00:05:57.214
The eval shows that merging the page reader with the writer hurts the rubric.

00:05:57.514 --> 00:06:01.971
The context fills up with web pages, and the report loses its sources.

00:06:02.271 --> 00:06:05.713
The team still wants the five job titles from the slide deck.

00:06:06.013 --> 00:06:07.802
What's the most useful next step?

00:06:08.102 --> 00:06:13.054
Keep only the measured split, the reader and the writer, and collapse the rest.

00:06:13.354 --> 00:06:17.101
The eval proved one technical reason between two roles.

00:06:17.401 --> 00:06:19.092
Context isolation.

00:06:19.392 --> 00:06:20.807
Not five job titles.

00:06:21.457 --> 00:06:24.337
And the classic trap, for someone starting out.

00:06:24.637 --> 00:06:28.397
Adding a reviewer, because a human team always has one.

00:06:28.697 --> 00:06:33.395
With no rubric and no eval, that reviewer learns to say approved.

00:06:33.695 --> 00:06:34.967
You paid for a stamp.

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:40.651
And if the sequence of steps is always fixed, search, then write, then format, you

00:06:40.651 --> 00:06:42.556
may not need an agent at all.

00:06:42.856 --> 00:06:47.222
A chain, with code in the middle, does it, and each step has a contract.

00:06:48.422 --> 00:06:49.908
Four quick cases.

00:06:50.208 --> 00:06:54.966
A pipeline with one agent that researches, and a second that writes from the

00:06:54.966 --> 00:06:56.406
sources, with no tools.

00:06:56.706 --> 00:07:00.028
And the eval shows that merging them hurts quality.

00:07:00.328 --> 00:07:01.355
Not this smell.

00:07:01.655 --> 00:07:04.291
The split has a measured technical reason.

00:07:04.941 --> 00:07:06.922
Three agents debate in rounds.

00:07:07.222 --> 00:07:10.490
The optimist, the pessimist and the moderator.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:16.088
No criterion, no eval, and the final answer is usually the moderator's first one.

00:07:16.388 --> 00:07:17.677
That is this smell.

00:07:17.977 --> 00:07:23.364
A debate is only justified with a judge, a written criterion, and an eval showing

00:07:23.364 --> 00:07:24.162
improvement.

00:07:24.812 --> 00:07:29.965
A main agent sends twenty subagents to explore twenty different repositories in

00:07:29.965 --> 00:07:33.095
parallel, and gets a one page summary from each.

00:07:33.395 --> 00:07:34.422
Not this smell.

00:07:34.722 --> 00:07:38.436
That's real, independent work, in separate contexts.

00:07:39.086 --> 00:07:44.186
And after collapsing, you add an evaluator with a written rubric, that only sees the

00:07:44.186 --> 00:07:46.372
final report, and the score goes up.

00:07:46.672 --> 00:07:48.084
Not this smell either.

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:51.239
That's independent review, with a measured gain.

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:54.643
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:54.943 --> 00:07:56.761
A model doesn't need an org chart.

00:07:57.061 --> 00:07:59.804
It needs the right tools and a clean context.

00:08:00.104 --> 00:08:01.886
Drop each agent and measure.

00:08:02.186 --> 00:08:07.175
Keep only the splits that move the score, name their technical reason, and collapse

00:08:07.175 --> 00:08:09.760
the rest into one agent that does the work.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:14.980
In the next lesson, we'll do that collapse step by step: collapse agents.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.151
See you there.
